{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RLVFgPvinzbznUEJa7L8hQ","type":"tool_result","content":"Scanned 36 files with 52 patterns: 11 hits written to ../agent/brace-prescan.json\n  B02: 1\n  B11: 4\n  E03: 1\n  R13: 5\nWrote ../agent/brace-inventory.json; candidate matches need manual scope review.\nagent/infra/terraform/main.tf\nagent/infra/terraform/github.tf\nagent/infra/terraform/iam.tf\nagent/test/controls.test.ts\nagent/test/budget.test.ts\nagent/brace-prescan.json\nagent/config/tools.allowlist.yaml\nagent/config/policy.yaml\nagent/config/budgets.yaml\nagent/Dockerfile\nagent/k8s/deployment.yaml\nagent/brace-inventory.json\nagent/release/manifest.json\nagent/brace-findings.json\nagent/README.md\nagent/package.json\nagent/.github/CODEOWNERS\nagent/.github/workflows/deploy.yml\nagent/.github/workflows/ci.yml\nagent/prompts/intent-classifier.txt\nagent/prompts/system.md\nagent/tsconfig.json\nagent/src/tools/executor.ts\nagent/src/tools/metadata-scan.ts\nagent/src/tools/registry.ts\nagent/src/tools/approvals.ts\nagent/src/workflows/eligibility-check.ts\nagent/src/agent/identity.ts\nagent/src/agent/context-size.ts\nagent/src/agent/loop.ts\nagent/src/agent/budget.ts\nagent/src/agent/classifier.ts\nagent/src/integrations/sms.ts\nagent/src/integrations/ehr-mcp.ts\nagent/src/integrations/payments.ts\nagent/src/integrations/eligibility-a2a.ts\nagent/src/audit/recorder.ts\nagent/src/workers/reminders.ts\nagent/src/config.ts\nagent/src/server.ts\nagent/src/routes/webchat.ts\nagent/src/routes/sms-webhook.ts\nagent/src/control/kill-switch.ts\n37:- A vendor service dropping agent or tenant identity from its records → **E10**\n38-- Tool or MCP server approved without vetting or pinning → **E02**\n39-\n40-### Heuristics\n41-\n42-**Provable from code:** partly\n43-\n44-**Look for:**\n45-- A dependency map listing outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, and audit services.\n46-- A responsibility table naming the agent-team owner and the platform or vendor owner for each applicable control, with evidence links for both.\n47-- Owner records in machine-readable form (service catalog, `.brace/deployment.yaml` roles, CODEOWNERS).\n48-\n49-**Red flags:**\n50-- Outside services in config (MCP servers, API base URLs, webhooks) that do not appear in any dependency or ownership list.\n51-- Controls marked N/A because an upstream vendor provides the service.\n52-- Vendor evidence assumed rather than linked, with no gap recorded.\n53-\n54-**Where to look:**\n55-- *.mcp.json, agent config, env vars with service URLs\n56-- service catalog files, architecture docs, `.brace/` records\n57-- IaC for gateways, identity providers, and log sinks\n58-\n59-**Needs runtime evidence:** Code shows declared dependencies; the E01 recipe compares them with a test trace of real calls and links evidence for each owner.\n60-\n61-**N/A when:** never\n62-\n63-## E02 · G2 · C3/C5 — Vet and pin dependencies\n64-\n65-**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Agent team, Model team, Vendors.\n66-\n67-### Requirement\n68-\n69-Before approving a tool or server, review its source code or available provenance, publisher, permissions, update path, and known vulnerabilities. Include model files, adapters, and datasets. Pin versions or digests, and verify signatures where they exist. A registry's namespace check shows who controls a publisher account, not that the code is safe. Record the limits of remote services and unsigned artifacts, and the extra checks you use instead.\n70-\n71-### Verification recipe\n72-\n73-For each approved dependency, including model files, adapters, and datasets, find the publisher, artifact and version, permissions, and update source. Verify signatures where they exist. Scan the pinned list for known vulnerabilities, and judge the findings under the team's release policy. Record missing provenance and how you reduce the risk.\n74-\n75-**Evidence to keep:** Dependency list, signature and scan results, review and exception records.\n76-\n77-### Checks for likely_pass\n--\n422:- Run can't be rebuilt across gateway, tool services, and delegated workers → **E10**\n423-\n424-### Heuristics\n425-\n426-**Provable from code:** partly\n427-\n428-**Look for:**\n429-- Every peer message authenticated (mTLS, signed requests, verified tokens) and the sender's scope checked.\n430-- Message type and schema validation for incoming A2A or peer requests.\n431-- Nonce, request ID, or timestamp tracking to stop replays where an action could repeat.\n432-- Signed agent cards verified, and card claims not used as authorization.\n433-- Callback and webhook URLs checked against private, link-local, and metadata addresses (SSRF).\n434-- Peer message content passed to the model as untrusted data.\n435-\n436-**Red flags:**\n437-- Peer requests accepted based on a valid signature alone, with no per-peer authorization.\n438-- Agent card skills or capabilities treated as permission grants.\n439-- Callback URLs fetched without SSRF checks (for example to `169.254.169.254`).\n440-- Retried peer requests re-run side effects with no idempotency key.\n441-- Peer message text inserted into the system prompt as instructions.\n442-\n443-**Where to look:**\n444-- A2A server handlers, agent card files (`/.well-known/agent-card.json`)\n445-- peer auth middleware\n446-- webhook and callback handlers\n447-- idempotency and replay protection code\n448-\n449-**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E07 fixtures: fake-sender, replayed, cross-tenant, tampered-card, and private-callback requests must be denied.\n450-\n451-**N/A when:** The agent receives no messages from other agents (no A2A server, peer endpoint, or agent-to-agent channel).\n452-\n453-## E08 · G1 · C2/C4 — Bounded delegated authority\n454-\n455-**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Security.\n456-\n457-### Requirement\n458-\n459-Give sub-agents only the powers approved for their task. Preserve the verified original subject and current actor through each handoff and enforce their task, tenant, resource, and audience limits where the action runs. Use supported token exchange or narrow task credentials rather than copying a parent's full credential. A parent must not gain a denied power by asking a more powerful peer to act without separate valid approval. For RFC 8693 tokens, earlier nested actors are provenance only and never add access. An actor chain or signed identity does not prove that a requested action was approved. Test legitimate delegation and that escalation attempt.\n460-\n461-  Why this is hard: A powerful peer may treat a low-privilege agent's request as a normal task. Knowing who sent the last message doesn't prove what the original requester was allowed to do. Delegation limits can also get lost across several hops.\n462-\n--\n561:- No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type → **E12**\n562-- Sub-agent given powers beyond its task → **E08**\n563-\n564-### Heuristics\n565-\n566-**Provable from code:** partly\n567-\n568-**Look for:**\n569-- A stop signal that spreads from root to all descendants (cancellation tokens, propagated stop messages).\n570-- Short-lived leases or credentials that children must renew, so disconnected workers lose access.\n571-- Queued work for a stopped tree is dropped or blocked; retries check a stop flag before running.\n572-- Open tool streams, subscriptions, and remote tasks are canceled on stop; a reconciliation step for effects already done.\n573-\n574-**Red flags:**\n575-- Stop only kills the parent process; children, queue workers, or remote tasks keep running.\n576-- Long-lived credentials given to remote workers.\n577-- Retry loops and schedulers with no check for a stopped run.\n578-- No time limit on tasks or subscriptions.\n579-\n580-**Where to look:**\n581-- orchestration and sub-agent lifecycle code\n582-- queue consumers and retry config\n583-- credential TTLs and lease logic\n584-- stop or kill endpoints and runbooks\n585-\n586-**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E09 drill across nested agents, a remote worker, queued work, and open streams, with stop and revocation times and denied retries.\n587-\n588-**N/A when:** never\n589-\n590:## E10 · G1 · C9/Obs-T1 — Audit across service boundaries\n591-\n592-**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Operations, Security, Vendors.\n593-\n594-### Requirement\n595-\n596-Rebuild a run across the gateway, tool services, and delegated workers, with all six identity fields kept. Check that the agent can't erase or rewrite its audit history, either directly or by asking a more powerful tool or peer. Check that a vendor boundary doesn't leave any action untraceable to the agent and instance that did it.\n597-\n598-  Why this is hard: Vendor services may drop parent or tenant identity, or return only request receipts. That leaves gaps between your gateway trace and what happened downstream. Shared credentials make attribution even weaker.\n599-\n600-  If you can't fully do it: Keep verified identity and correlation IDs at your gateway. Map them to vendor job or receipt IDs, and match them against any destination records you can get. Where identity can't be passed downstream, use separate, narrow credentials. Record which parts of the history you can't inspect. Gateway logs alone can't prove what happened inside those services. Limit or replace integrations whose evidence can't meet the attribution requirement. Don't mark the whole graph complete.\n601-\n602-  Tradeoffs you can accept: Extra integration work, slower reconciliation, and leaving out services you can't see into. Mapping verified identity to vendor receipts can support attribution once you've shown it works end to end. Actions that can never be traced still block release.\n603-\n604-### Verification recipe\n605-\n606-Run a task across the gateway, tools, and workers. Merge their logs into one graph with the six identity fields. Try to delete or edit records with the agent's credentials, and by asking a more powerful tool or peer to do it. Both must be denied, or held for approval under B05. Record any vendor segment you can't access as a gap.\n607-\n608-**Evidence to keep:** Cross-service graph, completeness query, audit-write denial.\n609-\n610-### Checks for likely_pass\n611-\n612-Rate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n613-\n614:- **E10.1** The gateway, tool services, and delegated workers each write audit records with all six identity fields and the shared trace context.\n615-  - *How to verify:* 1) Find audit writers in the gateway, tool services, and delegated workers. 2) Check each writes all six identity fields and the shared trace context. 3) Answer no if any component writes records without them or starts a new trace. Answer unknown if a component's code is outside this repo.\n616:- **E10.2** The agent's credentials have no permission to delete, update, or overwrite audit records (for example an append-only or WORM store with IAM denies).\n617-  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the agent's IAM role or database grants and the audit store config. 2) Check the agent has no delete, update, or overwrite rights (`DeleteObject`, overwriting `PutObject`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `TRUNCATE`) and the store is append-only or WORM (Object Lock). 3) Answer no if the agent's credentials can delete or rewrite records, or grant `*`. Answer unknown if these are set outside this repo.\n618:- **E10.3** No tool or peer the agent can call is able to delete or rewrite audit records on its behalf, or such requests are held for approval under B05.\n619-  - *How to verify:* 1) List the tools and peers the agent can call. 2) For each, check whether it can delete or rewrite audit records (database admin, log management, storage tools). 3) Answer no if any can and such requests are not held for approval under B05.\n620:- **E10.4** Calls into vendor services are mapped from the gateway's verified identity and correlation ID to the vendor's job or receipt ID.\n621-  - *How to verify:* 1) Find calls into vendor services. 2) Check the gateway stores its verified identity and correlation ID together with the vendor's job or receipt ID. 3) Answer no if the vendor ID is not stored, or stored without the link to identity and correlation ID.\n622-\n623-### Not covered here\n624-\n625-File these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n626-","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8c561077-96df-4d9c-ad99-669be22cb1e5","uuid":"a6bc4ae7-e4f0-4c2f-8710-beb332e6d528","timestamp":"2026-10-20T12:56:12.030Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"Scanned 36 files with 52 patterns: 11 hits written to ../agent/brace-prescan.json\n  B02: 1\n  B11: 4\n  E03: 1\n  R13: 5\nWrote ../agent/brace-inventory.json; candidate matches need manual scope review.\nagent/infra/terraform/main.tf\nagent/infra/terraform/github.tf\nagent/infra/terraform/iam.tf\nagent/test/controls.test.ts\nagent/test/budget.test.ts\nagent/brace-prescan.json\nagent/config/tools.allowlist.yaml\nagent/config/policy.yaml\nagent/config/budgets.yaml\nagent/Dockerfile\nagent/k8s/deployment.yaml\nagent/brace-inventory.json\nagent/release/manifest.json\nagent/brace-findings.json\nagent/README.md\nagent/package.json\nagent/.github/CODEOWNERS\nagent/.github/workflows/deploy.yml\nagent/.github/workflows/ci.yml\nagent/prompts/intent-classifier.txt\nagent/prompts/system.md\nagent/tsconfig.json\nagent/src/tools/executor.ts\nagent/src/tools/metadata-scan.ts\nagent/src/tools/registry.ts\nagent/src/tools/approvals.ts\nagent/src/workflows/eligibility-check.ts\nagent/src/agent/identity.ts\nagent/src/agent/context-size.ts\nagent/src/agent/loop.ts\nagent/src/agent/budget.ts\nagent/src/agent/classifier.ts\nagent/src/integrations/sms.ts\nagent/src/integrations/ehr-mcp.ts\nagent/src/integrations/payments.ts\nagent/src/integrations/eligibility-a2a.ts\nagent/src/audit/recorder.ts\nagent/src/workers/reminders.ts\nagent/src/config.ts\nagent/src/server.ts\nagent/src/routes/webchat.ts\nagent/src/routes/sms-webhook.ts\nagent/src/control/kill-switch.ts\n37:- A vendor service dropping agent or tenant identity from its records → **E10**\n38-- Tool or MCP server approved without vetting or pinning → **E02**\n39-\n40-### Heuristics\n41-\n42-**Provable from code:** partly\n43-\n44-**Look for:**\n45-- A dependency map listing outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, and audit services.\n46-- A responsibility table naming the agent-team owner and the platform or vendor owner for each applicable control, with evidence links for both.\n47-- Owner records in machine-readable form (service catalog, `.brace/deployment.yaml` roles, CODEOWNERS).\n48-\n49-**Red flags:**\n50-- Outside services in config (MCP servers, API base URLs, webhooks) that do not appear in any dependency or ownership list.\n51-- Controls marked N/A because an upstream vendor provides the service.\n52-- Vendor evidence assumed rather than linked, with no gap recorded.\n53-\n54-**Where to look:**\n55-- *.mcp.json, agent config, env vars with service URLs\n56-- service catalog files, architecture docs, `.brace/` records\n57-- IaC for gateways, identity providers, and log sinks\n58-\n59-**Needs runtime evidence:** Code shows declared dependencies; the E01 recipe compares them with a test trace of real calls and links evidence for each owner.\n60-\n61-**N/A when:** never\n62-\n63-## E02 · G2 · C3/C5 — Vet and pin dependencies\n64-\n65-**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Agent team, Model team, Vendors.\n66-\n67-### Requirement\n68-\n69-Before approving a tool or server, review its source code or available provenance, publisher, permissions, update path, and known vulnerabilities. Include model files, adapters, and datasets. Pin versions or digests, and verify signatures where they exist. A registry's namespace check shows who controls a publisher account, not that the code is safe. Record the limits of remote services and unsigned artifacts, and the extra checks you use instead.\n70-\n71-### Verification recipe\n72-\n73-For each approved dependency, including model files, adapters, and datasets, find the publisher, artifact and version, permissions, and update source. Verify signatures where they exist. Scan the pinned list for known vulnerabilities, and judge the findings under the team's release policy. Record missing provenance and how you reduce the risk.\n74-\n75-**Evidence to keep:** Dependency list, signature and scan results, review and exception records.\n76-\n77-### Checks for likely_pass\n--\n422:- Run can't be rebuilt across gateway, tool services, and delegated workers → **E10**\n423-\n424-### Heuristics\n425-\n426-**Provable from code:** partly\n427-\n428-**Look for:**\n429-- Every peer message authenticated (mTLS, signed requests, verified tokens) and the sender's scope checked.\n430-- Message type and schema validation for incoming A2A or peer requests.\n431-- Nonce, request ID, or timestamp tracking to stop replays where an action could repeat.\n432-- Signed agent cards verified, and card claims not used as authorization.\n433-- Callback and webhook URLs checked against private, link-local, and metadata addresses (SSRF).\n434-- Peer message content passed to the model as untrusted data.\n435-\n436-**Red flags:**\n437-- Peer requests accepted based on a valid signature alone, with no per-peer authorization.\n438-- Agent card skills or capabilities treated as permission grants.\n439-- Callback URLs fetched without SSRF checks (for example to `169.254.169.254`).\n440-- Retried peer requests re-run side effects with no idempotency key.\n441-- Peer message text inserted into the system prompt as instructions.\n442-\n443-**Where to look:**\n444-- A2A server handlers, agent card files (`/.well-known/agent-card.json`)\n445-- peer auth middleware\n446-- webhook and callback handlers\n447-- idempotency and replay protection code\n448-\n449-**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E07 fixtures: fake-sender, replayed, cross-tenant, tampered-card, and private-callback requests must be denied.\n450-\n451-**N/A when:** The agent receives no messages from other agents (no A2A server, peer endpoint, or agent-to-agent channel).\n452-\n453-## E08 · G1 · C2/C4 — Bounded delegated authority\n454-\n455-**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Security.\n456-\n457-### Requirement\n458-\n459-Give sub-agents only the powers approved for their task. Preserve the verified original subject and current actor through each handoff and enforce their task, tenant, resource, and audience limits where the action runs. Use supported token exchange or narrow task credentials rather than copying a parent's full credential. A parent must not gain a denied power by asking a more powerful peer to act without separate valid approval. For RFC 8693 tokens, earlier nested actors are provenance only and never add access. An actor chain or signed identity does not prove that a requested action was approved. Test legitimate delegation and that escalation attempt.\n460-\n461-  Why this is hard: A powerful peer may treat a low-privilege agent's request as a normal task. Knowing who sent the last message doesn't prove what the original requester was allowed to do. Delegation limits can also get lost across several hops.\n462-\n--\n561:- No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type → **E12**\n562-- Sub-agent given powers beyond its task → **E08**\n563-\n564-### Heuristics\n565-\n566-**Provable from code:** partly\n567-\n568-**Look for:**\n569-- A stop signal that spreads from root to all descendants (cancellation tokens, propagated stop messages).\n570-- Short-lived leases or credentials that children must renew, so disconnected workers lose access.\n571-- Queued work for a stopped tree is dropped or blocked; retries check a stop flag before running.\n572-- Open tool streams, subscriptions, and remote tasks are canceled on stop; a reconciliation step for effects already done.\n573-\n574-**Red flags:**\n575-- Stop only kills the parent process; children, queue workers, or remote tasks keep running.\n576-- Long-lived credentials given to remote workers.\n577-- Retry loops and schedulers with no check for a stopped run.\n578-- No time limit on tasks or subscriptions.\n579-\n580-**Where to look:**\n581-- orchestration and sub-agent lifecycle code\n582-- queue consumers and retry config\n583-- credential TTLs and lease logic\n584-- stop or kill endpoints and runbooks\n585-\n586-**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E09 drill across nested agents, a remote worker, queued work, and open streams, with stop and revocation times and denied retries.\n587-\n588-**N/A when:** never\n589-\n590:## E10 · G1 · C9/Obs-T1 — Audit across service boundaries\n591-\n592-**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Operations, Security, Vendors.\n593-\n594-### Requirement\n595-\n596-Rebuild a run across the gateway, tool services, and delegated workers, with all six identity fields kept. Check that the agent can't erase or rewrite its audit history, either directly or by asking a more powerful tool or peer. Check that a vendor boundary doesn't leave any action untraceable to the agent and instance that did it.\n597-\n598-  Why this is hard: Vendor services may drop parent or tenant identity, or return only request receipts. That leaves gaps between your gateway trace and what happened downstream. Shared credentials make attribution even weaker.\n599-\n600-  If you can't fully do it: Keep verified identity and correlation IDs at your gateway. Map them to vendor job or receipt IDs, and match them against any destination records you can get. Where identity can't be passed downstream, use separate, narrow credentials. Record which parts of the history you can't inspect. Gateway logs alone can't prove what happened inside those services. Limit or replace integrations whose evidence can't meet the attribution requirement. Don't mark the whole graph complete.\n601-\n602-  Tradeoffs you can accept: Extra integration work, slower reconciliation, and leaving out services you can't see into. Mapping verified identity to vendor receipts can support attribution once you've shown it works end to end. Actions that can never be traced still block release.\n603-\n604-### Verification recipe\n605-\n606-Run a task across the gateway, tools, and workers. Merge their logs into one graph with the six identity fields. Try to delete or edit records with the agent's credentials, and by asking a more powerful tool or peer to do it. Both must be denied, or held for approval under B05. Record any vendor segment you can't access as a gap.\n607-\n608-**Evidence to keep:** Cross-service graph, completeness query, audit-write denial.\n609-\n610-### Checks for likely_pass\n611-\n612-Rate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n613-\n614:- **E10.1** The gateway, tool services, and delegated workers each write audit records with all six identity fields and the shared trace context.\n615-  - *How to verify:* 1) Find audit writers in the gateway, tool services, and delegated workers. 2) Check each writes all six identity fields and the shared trace context. 3) Answer no if any component writes records without them or starts a new trace. Answer unknown if a component's code is outside this repo.\n616:- **E10.2** The agent's credentials have no permission to delete, update, or overwrite audit records (for example an append-only or WORM store with IAM denies).\n617-  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the agent's IAM role or database grants and the audit store config. 2) Check the agent has no delete, update, or overwrite rights (`DeleteObject`, overwriting `PutObject`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `TRUNCATE`) and the store is append-only or WORM (Object Lock). 3) Answer no if the agent's credentials can delete or rewrite records, or grant `*`. Answer unknown if these are set outside this repo.\n618:- **E10.3** No tool or peer the agent can call is able to delete or rewrite audit records on its behalf, or such requests are held for approval under B05.\n619-  - *How to verify:* 1) List the tools and peers the agent can call. 2) For each, check whether it can delete or rewrite audit records (database admin, log management, storage tools). 3) Answer no if any can and such requests are not held for approval under B05.\n620:- **E10.4** Calls into vendor services are mapped from the gateway's verified identity and correlation ID to the vendor's job or receipt ID.\n621-  - *How to verify:* 1) Find calls into vendor services. 2) Check the gateway stores its verified identity and correlation ID together with the vendor's job or receipt ID. 3) Answer no if the vendor ID is not stored, or stored without the link to identity and correlation ID.\n622-\n623-### Not covered here\n624-\n625-File these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n626-","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01RLVFgPvinzbznUEJa7L8hQ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgDXSZuVdK5a9hxfiwBbv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0191WHzQuZk6sopx9NymMY8Q","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n 626,760p skill/references/ecosystem.md; cat agent/brace-prescan.json; cat agent/brace-inventory.json | head -150"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4810,"cache_read_input_tokens":20512,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4810},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8c561077-96df-4d9c-ad99-669be22cb1e5","uuid":"e2781230-ad4f-484e-b5e9-d275243d2437","timestamp":"2026-10-20T12:56:16.236Z","request_id":"req_011CgDXSZZfUPRPvDdBAY54Q","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_0191WHzQuZk6sopx9NymMY8Q":{"command":"cd /private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-batch-ot33r5j4 && sed -n 626,760p skill/references/ecosystem.md; cat agent/brace-prescan.json; cat agent/brace-inventory.json | head -150"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_0191WHzQuZk6sopx9NymMY8Q":{"cwd":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-batch-ot33r5j4"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0191WHzQuZk6sopx9NymMY8Q","type":"tool_result","content":"- Audit retention, access, redaction, or export rules missing → **E11**\n- Authorization decisions missing the policy version in the run record → **R13**\n- Instance or tenant labels taken from what the model supplies → **A04**\n- Recovery checkpoints not tamper-evident → **R14**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Gateway, tool services, and workers all emit the six identity fields and propagate W3C trace context.\n- Gateway maps verified identity to vendor job or receipt IDs where identity can't pass downstream.\n- Audit logs go to a sink the agent can only append to (separate write-only role, WORM storage, S3 Object Lock in compliance mode).\n- No tool or peer the agent can call has delete or edit rights on audit storage.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Audit logs written by the agent's own credentials to a bucket it can delete from.\n- A tool or admin API the agent can call that can delete or rewrite logs.\n- Vendor calls made with shared credentials and no mapping from receipts back to agent and instance.\n- Trace context dropped at the gateway or at queue boundaries.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- audit log sink config and its IAM policy\n- bucket or storage settings (object lock, retention mode)\n- gateway logging and trace propagation\n- tool definitions with admin or log-management powers\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E10 test: merge logs across services into one graph with all six fields, and show deletes or edits by the agent, directly or through a more powerful tool or peer, are denied or held for approval.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## E11 · G2 · C9 — Protect shared evidence\n\n**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Governance, Data and privacy, Security, Vendors.\n\n### Requirement\n\nSet rules for how long audit records are kept, who can see them, what is redacted, how their integrity is checked, and how they are exported and restored. Test pulling up a past run, and test that missing records or a broken pipeline get noticed. Keep records long enough for investigations and for the law. For example, the EU AI Act requires providers and deployers of high-risk systems to keep automatically generated logs for at least six months (Articles 19 and 26). High-impact actions must fail closed when required audit capture isn't working.\n\n  Why this is hard: Audit delivery can fail while the agent keeps working. Buffers can fill up, records can arrive late, and a vendor may keep records for less time than you need. Exported logs may also hold sensitive data.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Use durable local buffering, or another protected audit path, with delivery receipts, backlog limits, and alerts for missing records. Export vendor evidence before the vendor deletes it, and limit who can see it. When buffering runs out or isn't available, pause affected actions before required evidence is lost. Record outages and any evidence you can't get back. A fixed pipeline doesn't bring back missing history.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Short delivery delays while records stay safely stored, extra storage and export costs, and paused work during outages. Limits on retention or delivery can get G2 acceptance only if the required G1 action capture and attribution still hold. Disclose any lost evidence.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nPull up an old test trace, check its integrity, and run the export and recovery procedure. Interrupt audit delivery and inject a missing event in the sequence. The gap must be caught, and high-impact actions must fail closed while capture is down. Check the retention period against the investigation window and the law that applies.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Retention and access policy, integrity and export results, outage alerts and response.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **E11.1** Audit retention is set in config to cover the investigation window and the legal minimum (for example at least six months for EU AI Act high-risk systems).\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find retention settings for audit storage: bucket lifecycle rules, log retention days, TTLs. 2) Check the value covers the investigation window and the legal minimum (for example six months for EU AI Act high-risk systems). 3) Answer no if retention is shorter or unset. Answer unknown if audit storage is configured outside this repo.\n- **E11.2** Access to audit records is limited by role, and redaction rules apply to sensitive fields.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find IAM policies or ACLs on audit storage. 2) Check reads are limited by role. 3) Check redaction rules apply to sensitive fields. 4) Answer no if read access is broad (`*`, all engineers) or no redaction exists.\n- **E11.3** Integrity protection (hash chain, signatures, or WORM) plus sequence numbers or delivery receipts let missing records be detected, with an alert on gaps or pipeline failure.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the audit writer and store config. 2) Check for integrity protection (hash chain, signatures, or WORM) plus sequence numbers or delivery receipts. 3) Check an alert fires on gaps or pipeline failure. 4) Answer no if any piece is missing, or a hash is computed but never verified.\n- **E11.4** High-impact actions are blocked when required audit capture is down.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find high-impact action paths. 2) Check they confirm audit capture is working before the action runs. 3) Answer no if audit write errors are caught and the action proceeds, or the audit record is written only after success.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Agent able to erase or rewrite its own audit history → **E10**\n- Run record missing denials, approvals, or results → **R13**\n- Secrets left in tool output, responses, or normal logs → **R03**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Retention set to cover the investigation window and legal needs (for example at least six months for EU AI Act high-risk logs).\n- Integrity checks on audit records (hash chains, signed batches, sequence numbers).\n- Durable local buffering with delivery receipts, backlog limits, and alerts on gaps.\n- High-impact actions fail closed when audit capture is down.\n- Documented export, restore, redaction, and access rules.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Audit writes are fire-and-forget; failures are logged and the action continues.\n- Default backend retention (for example 7 or 30 days) with no longer archive.\n- No alert when log delivery stops or sequence numbers skip.\n- Exported logs hold unredacted sensitive data with broad read access.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- audit logging client and buffering code\n- log backend and bucket lifecycle settings\n- monitoring and alert rules for log pipelines\n- data retention and redaction policy docs\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E11 recipe: restore an old trace, check integrity, interrupt delivery and inject a missing event, and confirm the gap is caught and high-impact actions stop.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## E12 · G3 · C7/C8 — Fleet incident response\n\n**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Operations, Governance, Business owner.\n\n### Requirement\n\nRun a drill where a shared tool or agent type is compromised. Find the affected instances and tenants. Quarantine the dependency, revoke the access involved, stop affected work, and alert the named responders. Check that alerts about abuse across agents, and about budgets, reach the team that can act. Name who decides whether to report outside the organization and share information.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nMark a shared test tool or version as compromised. Query which instances and tenants it affects, quarantine it, revoke the access involved, and trigger response alerts. Affected work must stop within the set targets. Responders must get identities and traces they can act on. Record who decided on outside reporting and information sharing.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Fleet impact query, quarantine and revocation records, alert delivery and response times.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nChecks for likely_pass:\n\n- (none) Code can't show this item. Don't rate it likely_pass; use needs_evidence, gap, or na_candidate.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Kill switch for a single agent not drilled → **R11**\n- No owner named on each side of a shared control → **E01**\n- Tool or MCP server approved without vetting or pinning → **E02**\n- Budget and rate limits not enforced outside the model → **B07**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** no\n\n**Look for:**\n- An inventory or query that maps tool and dependency versions to instances and tenants.\n{\n  \"repo\": \"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-batch-ot33r5j4/agent\",\n  \"patterns_file\": \"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-batch-ot33r5j4/skill/prescan/patterns.yaml\",\n  \"files_scanned\": 36,\n  \"note\": \"Hits are places to read, not findings. Answer each hit's ask before citing it.\",\n  \"capped\": [],\n  \"hits\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"H001\",\n      \"pattern\": \"P27\",\n      \"item\": \"B11\",\n      \"title\": \"securityContext to check for missing hardening\",\n      \"file\": \"k8s/deployment.yaml\",\n      \"line\": 67,\n      \"text\": \"securityContext:\",\n      \"ask\": \"Does this securityContext set allowPrivilegeEscalation: false, runAsNonRoot: true, readOnlyRootFilesystem: true, and drop ALL capabilities? A missing field defaults to the permissive value.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"H002\",\n      \"pattern\": \"P27\",\n      \"item\": \"B11\",\n      \"title\": \"securityCo